        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Sorry for hijacking the thread. Here's another trigger for the same bug: in a dark terminal, `:colorscheme github_light_default` (github-nvim-theme) followed by Ctrl-Z / `fg` drops me back to the default colorscheme. Here's what Claude Code found when digging into it:\n\ngithub-nvim-theme's `colors/*.vim` files set `'background'` through `:lua` (`lua require('github-theme').load({ theme = 'github_light_default' })`, the same for every variant). So a plain `:colorscheme`, with no special startup setup, records `'background'` with `last_set_sid = -8`, and `bg_user_set()` treats the theme's choice as an auto-detected value.\n\nAfter Ctrl-Z / `fg`, the OSC 11 reply sets `'background'` to `dark`. That reloads the colorscheme, which sets `light` again, and the `'background'` handling then gives up on it: `g:colors_name` is unset and the default highlights come back. So the outcome is worse than in the original report: the whole colorscheme is lost on every resume, not just the option value.\n\nMinimal repro on `v0.13.0-dev-1721+g7dbca1c4e2`, in a dark terminal that answers OSC 11:\n\n```vim\n\" /tmp/repro/colors/lightonly.vim\nhi clear\nlet g:colors_name = 'lightonly'\nlua vim.o.background = 'light'\nhi Normal guifg=#000000 guibg=#ffffff\n```\n\n1. `nvim --clean --cmd 'set rtp^=/tmp/repro'`, then `:colorscheme lightonly`\n2. `:lua =vim.api.nvim_get_option_info2('background').last_set_sid` prints `-8`\n3. Ctrl-Z, then `fg`\n4. `:echo get(g:, 'colors_name', 'unset') &background` prints `unset dark`\n\nThe same scheme written as `colors/lightonly.lua` records the file's script ID and survives. So a fix limited to `VIMINIT`/`--cmd` wouldn't cover this case; a dedicated SID for the detection's own writes, as proposed in #40647, would.\n\nThat half of #40647 could land on its own: if `bg_user_set()` checks for the detection's dedicated SID instead of `SID_LUA`, Lua writes from `VIMINIT`, `--cmd` and colorschemes all count as user-set, without changing what `SID_LUA` means anywhere else. The other half, re-labelling Lua chunks by their source, is what raised the `writefile()` and `luaeval()`/`v:lua` concerns.\n\nWorkaround until then, in `init.lua`:\n\n```lua\nvim.api.nvim_create_autocmd(\"ColorScheme\", {\n  callback = function() vim.o.background = vim.o.background end,\n})\n```",
            "created_at": "2026-10-20T09:05:48Z",
